<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Milovan Djilas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1911">1911</a>–<a href="/years/1995">1995</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Milovan Đilas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Milovan Djilas was a Yugoslav communist politician, theorist and author, born near Mojkovac in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>. Jailed and tortured as a student radical in the 1930s, he joined the politburo of the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> in 1939, helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Josip Broz Tito</a> build the Partisan resistance in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and became vice-president in Tito's government. Once regarded as Tito's possible successor, he was expelled from the Central Committee in January 1954 after articles arguing against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a>. In The New Class, published abroad in 1957, he argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> had created a privileged class of party bureaucracy, and he spent years in prison for his writings.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/milovan-djilas.jpg" alt="Milovan Djilas" width="330" height="451" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stevan Kragujević · CC BY-SA 3.0 rs · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stevan_Kragujevic,_Milovan_Djilas,1950.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Milovan Djilas was born on 12 June 1911 at Podbišće near Mojkovac in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a>, the fourth of nine children in a peasant family. His father Nikola had served in the Montenegrin Army and opposed the incorporation of Montenegro into the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a>. Djilas began studying literature at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> in 1929, already a committed communist. In March 1932 he was jailed for eight days for an anti-government demonstration. Arrested again eleven months later, he was tortured and sentenced to three years in Sremska Mitrovica Prison, where he met senior party members and became a committed Stalinist.</p><p>After his release in 1936 he gave up study for the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Josip Broz Tito</a> against Stalin's attempts to control it, and entered the Central Committee in 1938 and the politburo the next year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> in April 1941, Djilas helped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a> establish the Partisan resistance. The party resolved to begin an uprising on 4 July, and he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montenegro">Montenegro</a> to organise it. In November 1941 Tito dismissed him from command there for his errors, including what were called leftist errors, and made him editor of the party paper Borba. In March 1944 he went on a mission to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and met Stalin. After the war he became vice-president in Tito's government, and in 1948 he was sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in an attempt to bridge the gap between Moscow and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>. As the break came, he became one of the leading critics of Stalin's efforts to control Yugoslavia.</p><p>Djilas was widely seen as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a>'s possible successor, and he held the post of President of the Federal People's Assembly from 25 December 1953 to 28 January 1954. In his Borba articles of that winter he criticised bureaucratic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stalinism">Stalinism</a> and went on to argue against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/one-party-state">one-party state</a> itself. He was expelled from the Central Committee in January 1954 and resigned from the League of Communists in March.</p><p>He was sentenced in 1956 for backing the Hungarian Revolution, and in 1957 for The New Class, published abroad, which argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> was creating a new class of privileged party bureaucrats. It was translated into more than 40 languages. Conversations with Stalin brought a further sentence in 1962. In prison he wrote a biography of Njegoš and translated Paradise Lost into Serbo-Croatian. He was freed by amnesty on 31 December 1966.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Djilas stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> as a dissident. He opposed the breakup of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, though he predicted in the 1980s that it would come on lines of bureaucratic nationalism after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a>. He died on 20 April 1995.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Milovan Djilas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Milovan Djilas</b>, once among the leaders of communist Yugoslavia, argued in The New Class (1957) that the party bureaucracy had become a new privileged ruling class, and his prison sentence was lengthened for publishing the book abroad.</blockquote><a href="/government/one-party-state">One-party state · Weaknesses</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Podbišće, in what is now <a href="/countries/montenegro">Montenegro</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.94,19.57&amp;year=1965" aria-label="Podbišće on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5023 639 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Milovan Djilas"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5473 909h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5473 909h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.94,19.57&amp;year=1965">What was here in 1965? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
